# 1. MAIN OBJECTIVE

Implement a complete, playable, real-time automatic battle system.

The player must be able to:

1. Enter a battle from the existing game interface.
2. Arrange the initial army before battle.
3. Start the battle.
4. Watch friendly and enemy units move independently.
5. Observe automatic target selection and combat.
6. Deploy units using tactical cards.
7. Use skill, tactic, and support cards.
8. Merge identical adjacent cards.
9. Manage Command Energy.
10. Activate the commander's ultimate ability.
11. Fight multiple enemy waves.
12. Defeat elite enemies and bosses.
13. Win or lose the battle.
14. Receive rewards.
15. Return to the existing game interface.

Every visible control must perform its advertised action.

No fake buttons, decorative-only mechanics, or placeholder combat outcomes.

# 2. BATTLEFIELD

## 2.1 Camera and map

Use a 2D isometric battlefield.

The battlefield is a finite square arena.

Use a world coordinate system of approximately 100 × 100 units.

The screen projection must create an isometric diagonal view.

Important distinction:

- Movement and combat calculations use world coordinates.
- Rendering uses isometric screen projection.
- Do not calculate movement directly in screen coordinates.

Friendly units spawn from the LEFT side.

Enemy units spawn from the RIGHT side.

There are no bases.

There is no base HP.

There are no base attacks.

There are no base destruction victory conditions.

## 2.2 Battlefield regions

Divide the arena logically into:

- Friendly deployment region
- Central combat region
- Enemy initial deployment region

Friendly initial deployment and reinforcements must respect the friendly deployment boundary.

Enemy units spawn within their designated region.

Units can move freely throughout all traversable battlefield space.

Do not constrain combat to lanes or fixed paths.

## 2.3 Terrain

Implement a practical terrain system.

Support:

- Walkable ground
- Obstacles
- Blocked terrain
- Optional decorative terrain

Obstacles must affect pathfinding and movement.

Decorative terrain must not block units unless explicitly configured.

Use data-driven terrain configuration.

## 2.4 Rendering

Implement:

- Isometric terrain rendering
- Unit rendering
- Shadows
- Health bars
- Team identification
- Selection or target indicators
- Projectile rendering
- Skill effects
- Damage feedback
- Death animations
- Commander ultimate effects

Sort units by isometric depth so that foreground units correctly overlap background units.

Use the existing AssetManager.

Missing art assets must use graceful placeholders.

Do not block gameplay because final art assets are unavailable.

# 3. UNIT SYSTEM

## 3.1 Unit limit

Maximum active combat units:

- Friendly: 25
- Enemy: 25
- Total: 50

Enforce these limits consistently.

Do not silently create unlimited units.

## 3.2 Core unit classes

Implement the following four fully functional classes.

### Heavy Defender — 重装盾卫

Role: frontline defense.

Characteristics:

- High HP
- High defense
- Low movement speed
- Melee attack
- Prioritizes nearby enemy frontline units
- Protects nearby friendly units where practical

### Swift Cavalry — 疾风骑兵

Role: mobile assault.

Characteristics:

- High movement speed
- High melee damage
- Lower durability than Heavy Defender
- Prioritizes enemy ranged units and vulnerable targets
- Attempts to approach targets from the side when practical

### Arcane Archer — 奥术射手

Role: ranged damage.

Characteristics:

- Ranged attacks
- Projectile-based damage
- Maintains a preferred attack distance
- Prioritizes high-threat enemies
- Avoids unnecessarily approaching melee enemies

### Field Cleric — 战地圣职

Role: healing support.

Characteristics:

- Searches for injured friendly units
- Prioritizes valuable or critically injured allies
- Heals allies within healing range
- Attacks enemies if no valid healing target exists
- Must not heal defeated units

